<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Changing Forests – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Explored the Ecological History Of...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the linear structure of a short formal talk. Interactive: Respond to questions and arguments in debates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow the linear sequence of points on a short history of local ecology (e.g., "You can see from the photo in 2020 there was more rainforest in the area than in 2025.")
Can respond to questions in arguments or debates on forest change (e.g., "The author makes a good argument about cutting down the forest for farming. It has a big impact on the environment.")
Can identify and discuss key points in a structured talk on ecological issues (e.g., "The first part of the talk was about the water cycle of rainforests.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of contrasting images (e.g., a lush, biodiverse rainforest vs. a deforested area with logging equipment, or a pristine river vs. a river with plastic waste). Students work in pairs to describe what they see in each image, using initial thoughts on environmental impact. Teacher introduces the scenario "Our Changing Forests."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "deforestation," "biodiversity," "pollution," "conservation," "ecological," "dramatic," "climate," "life," "type" (emphasizing /aɪ/ and /ɪt/ sounds and word stress). The teacher uses gestures or synonyms to clarify meaning, for example, showing a picture of many different plants and animals for "biodiversity" or pretending to cut down a tree for "logging."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a forest has high biodiversity, does it have many different types of plants and animals, or just a few?" (Expected answer: Many different types.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we talk about 'deforestation,' are we cutting down trees or planting them?" (Expected answer: Cutting down trees.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording (approx. 2 minutes) of a short formal talk by an environmental expert discussing the ecological history of a local Panamanian forest (e.g., "The Changing Face of Altos de Campana National Park"). The talk will naturally incorporate target vocabulary (e.g., "deforestation," "biodiversity," "conservation") and grammar structures (e.g., "Deforestation *has significantly affected* biodiversity in our area," "If we *preserve* our forests, we *will help reduce* pollution").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the linear structure of a short formal talk.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the talk once and answer a general question: "What is the main topic of the expert's talk?" (e.g., "The history of changes in a local forest and its challenges.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise based on specific points in the talk. E.g., "The talk mentioned that biodiversity in the park has increased over the last decade. (T/F)" or "According to the speaker, illegal logging has been a significant problem. (T/F)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Information Matching):</b> Students listen for key dates or events mentioned in the linear structure of the talk and match them (e.g., Match "1980s" with "beginning of significant deforestation" or "Present day" with "conservation efforts and community involvement").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific grammar (Present Perfect, First and Second Conditional, Comparatives) and target vocabulary (deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, conservation, impact, protect, recover, significant, ecological).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Listening & Cloze):</b> The teacher provides a transcript of short excerpts from the audio talk with specific target vocabulary words (nouns, verbs, adjectives) removed. Students listen to the original audio again and fill in the blanks.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Over the past few decades, _________ (deforestation) has had a _________ (dramatic) _________ (impact) on local _________ (biodiversity)."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listening for Grammar Patterns):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences from the talk again. Students listen and identify the grammatical structure used (Present Perfect, First Conditional, Second Conditional, or Comparative). They write down the sentence and label the structure.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: Teacher plays: "Deforestation *has significantly affected* biodiversity in our area." Students write: "Present Perfect."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: Teacher plays: "If we *preserve* our forests, we *will help reduce* pollution." Students write: "First Conditional."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: Teacher plays: "Urban ecosystems *have less biodiversity compared to* rural habitats." Students write: "Comparative."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listening for Argument Points):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays short, distinct arguments or statements from the talk related to causes or effects of forest change (e.g., "The author makes a good argument about cutting down the forest for farming. It has a big impact on the environment."). Each pair listens and identifies the key argument or point being made, then briefly discusses which vocabulary words were used to convey the message.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided mini-debate/discussion. Students will participate in a "Forest Future Forum" where they respond to hypothetical scenarios and arguments related to the listened talk, focusing on expressing environmental concerns politely and discussing conservation ideas. The teacher acts as a moderator.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher presents 2-3 short, provocative statements or questions based on the formal talk and the scenario "Our Changing Forests." These statements will explicitly prompt the use of target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "The talk highlighted the impact of logging. How *has deforestation affected* air quality in our area, based on what we've learned?" (Pragmatic: Asking about environmental impact; Grammar: Present Perfect)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "Some say if we stopped all development near forests, our economy would suffer. What are your thoughts? If we *planted* more trees, *would* forests *recover* faster?" (Pragmatic: Discussing conservation ideas; Sociolinguistic: Expressing concerns politely; Grammar: Second Conditional)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: "Considering the talk, are urban ecosystems *healthier or less diverse compared to* natural habitats? And if we *reduce plastic waste*, *will* it *improve* marine life?" (Grammar: Comparatives, First Conditional; Vocabulary: marine life, plastic waste, healthier, improve)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns responding to these prompts, engaging in a structured, polite discussion. The teacher encourages active listening and direct responses to peers' points. Students are prompted to use phrases like "I agree with [name] that..." or "I respectfully disagree because..." and incorporate the target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays one final short, structured audio statement (approx. 45 seconds) related to forest conservation efforts. Students then individually write down 2-3 key points they understood about the linear sequence of the message and participate in a quick whole-class discussion, answering one question about the speaker's main proposal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow the linear structure of the talk (identifies main points in sequence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (deforestation, biodiversity, conservation, impact, protect, recover) in responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /aɪ/ and /ɪt/ sounds and correct word stress in multi-syllabic words during discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of responses to questions and arguments.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and respectful interaction with peers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase I learned today about 'Our Changing Forests' is __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am now more confident in understanding talks about ecological issues because __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did I face today while listening to the talk, and how did I try to overcome them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can understanding the ecological history of our forests help me discuss current environmental issues?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The listening activities were effective in introducing new vocabulary and concepts. Students responded well to the controlled practice and pair work, demonstrating good initial comprehension of the talk's linear structure. Pronunciation of /aɪ/ and word stress needs more targeted practice for some students. The mini-debate provided a good opportunity for initial production, but some struggled with spontaneously forming complex sentences using the conditional structures. Next time, I will provide more explicit sentence starters for conditional sentences. For Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a text that reinforces the same vocabulary and grammar, deepening their contextual understanding. I will ensure the reading material builds directly on the ecological history theme introduced today, providing a textual complement to the audio input.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>